  "report": "The data contain a one-dimensional closed curve hidden by modular mixing. The file actually has 4000 headerless rows and 50 columns; all 50 were authorized for analysis. Two sums of six columns each recover the curve; the remaining 38 columns are not needed for this recovery. The map centers and unwraps both coordinates so the curve never crosses a wrapping border. Its shape is a smooth, slightly asymmetric rounded diamond, with no filled interior and no measurable thickness. More precisely, for 0<=t<2*pi, its coordinates are approximately u=0.24*cos(t)+0.005436206328*cos(2*t)+0.003740070342*sin(2*t)+0.032966152618*cos(3*t)+0.020253455423*sin(3*t), v=0.24*sin(t)-0.014686449995*cos(2*t)+0.009499503341*sin(2*t)+0.012354584070*cos(3*t)-0.013134607360*sin(3*t). Coefficients were fitted on 500 rows; all 3500 other rows lie within 1.06e-15 of this curve. Single-column summaries, pairwise dependence tests and PCA looked like uniform noise. A sparse Fourier search through modular combinations involving up to six columns found two independent signals, each with amplitude about 0.5, confirmed on independent rows. A 200-vertex closed polyline approximates the fitted curve; maximum held-out distance to this drawing is 2.33e-05. Of 100000 independent uniform 50-column noise rows passed through the same map, 0.027% lie within 0.0001 of the drawing, compared with 100% of held-out data. Scripts, diagnostic plots, coefficients and numerical validation are saved in analysis_output. The drawing contains only the outline; triangles are intentionally empty."
